  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Johnson vs Food Inspector on 7 December, 2017 Author: P.Ubaid Bench: P.Ubaid In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice P.Ubaid Thursday, The 7Th Day Of December 2017/16Th Agrahayana, 1939 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3043 of 2003 ( ) --------------------------------- Against The Judgment In Cra 614/2001 of Iii Additional Sessions Court, (Adhoc), Thrissur Against The Judgment In St 7026/1997 of J.M.F.C. - I, Chalakudy Revision Petitioner/Appellant/Accused(S): --------------------------------------------------- Johnson,S/O.Cherian, Chungath House, Muringoor P.O, Thrissur District. By Advs.Sri.Santhosh Subrahmanian Sri.Sheji P.Abraham Respondent/Complainant/State: ---------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Chalakudy Municipality, Chalakudy P.O. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor Sri.C.M.Kammappu Johnson vs Food Inspector on 7 December, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/51490474/ 1 This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 07-12-2017, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: P.Ubaid, J. ~~~~~~~~~~ Crl.R.P No.3043 of 2003 ~~~~~~~~~~~ Dated this the 7th December, 2017 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The revision petitioner herein challenges the conviction and sentence against him under Section 16(1) (c) (d) read with Section 10(2) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act (P.F.A Act) and Rule 12 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules in S.T 7026/1997 of the Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Chalakudy. He is the licensee of the grocery shop being run in the building No.22/231 of the Chalakudy Municipality. The said prosecution was brought against the revision petitioner by the Food Inspector of the Chalakudy Municipality, on the allegation that when the Food Inspector visited the shop of the revision petitioner at about 11.30 a.m on 15.11.1997 for collecting sample of Bengal-gram (channa) for analysis, he was obstructed and threatened by the revision petitioner, and when the Food Inspector demanded 750 gms of Bengal-gram, the revision petitioner declined the request. According to the Food Inspector, he had also prepared the Form VI notice to be given to the accused. 2. The accused appeared before the learned Magistrate and pleaded not guilty when the substance of the accusation was read over and explained to him. The Food Inspector examined himself and also his Office Peon, and proved Exts.P1 to P6 documents. The accused denied the incriminating circumstances when examined under Section 313 Cr.P.C. He did not adduce any evidence in defence. Aggrieved by the judgment of conviction, the accused approached the Court of session, Thrissur with Crl.A 614/2001.",
